 "standard_text": "8.2D order a set of real numbers arising from mathematical and real-world contexts",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 8 worksheets for Texas TEKS 8.2D: compare and order real numbers including square roots, π, fractions and decimals, numbers between two values and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Change every number to a decimal, then compare.",
 "key_sub": "π ≈ 3.14 < √10 ≈ 3.16 < 3.2 < 3 1/4.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Write every number as a decimal approximation: √10 ≈ 3.16, π ≈ 3.14, 3 1/4 = 3.25.</li><li>Then compare the decimals, or place them on a number line.</li><li>For negative numbers, the one farther from 0 is less: −√10 &lt; −3.</li><li>A square root √n is between the square roots of the perfect squares on either side.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Order √10, π, 3 1/4, 3.2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3.16, 3.14, 3.25, 3.2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: π, √10, 3.2, 3 1/4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comparing √n with n</td><td>√10 ≈ 3.16, not 10.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting negatives reverse the order</td><td>−√10 ≈ −3.16 is less than −3.</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering by the digits only</td><td>Change every number to a decimal first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does TEKS 8.2D ask?",
   "a": "Order a set of real numbers arising from mathematical and real-world contexts."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is √10 greater than π?",
   "a": "Yes. √10 ≈ 3.162 and π ≈ 3.142."
  },
  {
   "q": "How many decimal places do I need?",
   "a": "Use enough places to tell the numbers apart, usually two."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can I use a number line?",
   "a": "Yes. Place each approximation, then read from left to right."
  }
